### 7.2 The Architecture Gap: How "My Blog Desk" Must Operate

The companion app envisions **"committing content files only (Markdown/MDX/JSON/images) via the GitHub API without touching site code"**.

To make this possible, you have two clear implementation paths:

#### Option A: Introduce a File-Based Content Layer to the Website (Recommended)
Before "My Blog Desk" goes live, perform a one-time refactor of the repository:
1. Create a root `content/` folder:
   * `content/soul-notes/<slug>.json` (or `.md` with frontmatter)
   * `content/musings/<slug>.json`
   * `content/heartlines/<slug>.json`
   * `content/series/<seriesSlug>/<chapterSlug>.json`
   * `content/gallery.json`
2. Replace hardcoded arrays in `src/app/` with standard Node.js `fs.readdirSync` / `fs.readFileSync` calls (converting routes to Next.js Server Components with `generateStaticParams()`).
3. After this refactor, "My Blog Desk" only ever needs to POST a single `.json` or `.md` file to `content/...` and upload images to `public/...`. Vercel will auto-deploy smoothly.

#### Option B: If the Website Must Remain Untouched Today (Headless Git Committer)
If the website code cannot be refactored first, the "My Blog Desk" backend or client **must act as an AST / File Rewriter** using the GitHub Contents API:
1. Fetch the target file (e.g. `src/app/soul-notes/page.tsx` and `src/app/soul-notes/[slug]/page.tsx`).
2. Parse the TypeScript AST or inject the new object into the exported array/dictionary.
3. Commit the modified `.tsx` file back to GitHub.
*(Note: Option B is significantly more fragile than Option A, as any syntax typo will fail the Vercel build).*

